      quotes: [
        { text: "Never sell a product or service. Always sell a concept.", page: "71" },
        { text: "You sell the sizzle and not the steak—the concept and not the product.", page: "71" }
      ],
      idea: "Sugarman calls this one of the most important and basic principles he teaches: learn this single point and you've mastered a major lesson. It's **Axiom 9**.\n\n**What a concept is.** The buzzword changes: *positioning*, *Big Idea*, *USP* (unique selling proposition), even *gimmick*. They all mean the same thing: the angle that places the product in the prospect's mind. Sizzle, not steak.\n\n**The one exception.** When a product is so new or unique that the product itself is the concept. When digital watches first came out, he could hardly keep them in stock: he explained the new features and took orders. Once watches were everywhere and everyone understood them, each ad needed a concept to stand apart: the world's thinnest, the one with a built-in alarm, the most expensive band, the finest quality, even one that needed a laser beam to manufacture. From then on the concept, not the watch, made the sale.\n\n**Concepts that live in the name.**\n- **Pocket CB.** There were walkie-talkies and there were mobile CB radios; this was the first CB in your pocket. The name was the concept.\n- **Pocket Yellow Pages** (Chapter 11). He didn't sell a calculator; he sold the moment at the phone booth when you pull out an electronic directory and amaze the people around you.\n- **“Nose.”** A smoke detector sold as something that sits on your ceiling and sniffs the air. It sold quite well.\n\n**Concepts can be created, not just found.** Two products, a small travel alarm clock and a chess computer, sold well in his catalog with little copy. Instead of giving each its own full-page ad, he put both in one ad headlined **“Winners”**: these were the top sellers from his latest catalog. One concept carried both products and promoted the catalog at the same time.\n\n**Tcudorp.** He closes with a favorite ad from the Leo Burnett agency that ran in *Advertising Age*, headlined with the word *product* spelled backwards. Its point: an agency's first job is to turn your product over in every direction (front to back, side to side, upside down, inside out), because the drama that will sell it is hidden in the product itself. There are thousands of ways to stage that drama, and in a market crowded with me-too products it's harder to find, but the ad insists every good product contains it and a good agency always digs it out. It ends with a joke that the *t* is silent. Sugarman agrees: every product has something that makes it stand out, and it's the copywriter's job to dig it out. The right position can separate a smash hit from a flop.\n\nHow do you find it? That's the next chapter (incubation), and Chapter 16's problem restatements. And it depends on Chapter 2: you can't find the drama in a product you haven't studied.",
